@* Written in each component, not in an _Imports.razor, whose class Razor makes public (ADR-0201).
   Every product using is written with global::. The component lives in ExPivot.Components, where a
   plain "ExPivot.Engine" could be looked up against the ExPivot component class first, and a plain
   "ExGrid.X" against sibling types (the CS0426 trap in AGENTS.md). The Web namespace makes @on...
   handlers compile as event handlers rather than as plain HTML attributes. *@
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Web
@using global::ExGrid
@using global::ExGrid.Cells
@using global::ExGrid.Chrome
@using global::ExGrid.Columns
@using global::ExGrid.Components
@using global::ExGrid.Rows
@using global::ExGrid.Selection
@using global::ExPivot.Chrome
@using global::ExPivot.Engine
@* Not the contract (ADR-0201): ExPivot's built-in parts place this button in their own markup, and
   no Consumer places it. It is public only because Razor makes every component public, is hidden
   from a Consumer's editor, and may change in any version. *@
@attribute [System.ComponentModel.EditorBrowsable(System.ComponentModel.EditorBrowsableState.Never)]
@* A button that takes DOM focus when ExPivot asks (ADR-0061): once for each request number it
   has not acted on, as a template's control does (ADR-0037). The built-in views put the keyboard
   back on an entry this way when its menu or panel closes. Where the keyboard comes from the
   report's grid — the dialog's Close, a details tab — it is taken through TakeKeyboard, which the
   grid grants only while the keyboard is still the report's (ADR-0070's note of 2026-10-02). *@
<button @ref="_button" type="button" class="@Class" disabled="@Disabled" @onclick="OnClick" @attributes="Attributes">@ChildContent</button>

@code {
    private ElementReference _button;
    private int _acted;

    /// <summary>The button's classes.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether the button is disabled.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Disabled { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Pressed.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnClick { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The request to take DOM focus; zero asks nothing.</summary>
    [Parameter] public int FocusRequest { get; set; }

    /// <summary>How the button takes DOM focus when asked: called with its element, in place of the
    /// element's own <c>FocusAsync</c> — the report's grid handing the keyboard on (ADR-0070's note
    /// of 2026-10-02). Null, the element's own focus.</summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<ElementReference, Task>? TakeKeyboard { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What the button shows.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Any other attribute, on the button.</summary>
    [Parameter(CaptureUnmatchedValues = true)] public IReadOnlyDictionary<string, object>? Attributes { get; set; }

    /// <inheritdoc />
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (FocusRequest != 0 && FocusRequest != _acted)
        {
            _acted = FocusRequest;
            await (TakeKeyboard is { } take ? take(_button) : _button.FocusAsync().AsTask());
        }
    }
}
